WEBVTT

1
00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.695
[ Dua Lipa "End Of An Era" playing]

2
00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:36.578
♪ One. two. three. ayy

3
00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:40.040
♪ What's it about a kiss

4
00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:42.209
That makes me feel like this? ♪♪

5
00:00:42.292 --> 00:00:46.129
♪ Makes me an optimist
I guess

6
00:00:46.213 --> 00:00:50.175
I always jump too quick
Hopin' this one might stick♪

7
00:00:50.259 --> 00:00:52.302
♪Hopelessly romantic

8
00:00:52.386 --> 00:00:55.931
♪Then you said "Hey" ת

9
00:00:56.014 --> 00:01:00.185
♪ And I said "Hey"

10
00:01:00.269 --> 00:01:04.231
♪ What's your name?♪

11
00:01:04.314 --> 00:01:05.983
♪Come with me♪

12
00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:07.442
-[horn honks]
-[gasps] Whoa!

13
00:01:07.526 --> 00:01:12.447
♪'Cause when I see your face
Ah

14
00:01:12.531 --> 00:01:15.993
♪ The sweetest pleasure ♪

15
00:01:16.076 --> 00:01:20.247
♪ I feel like we're gonna be together ♪

16
00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:24.251
♪This could be the end of an era♪

17
00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:28.005
♪Who knows. baby
This could be forever

18
00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:30.048
♪Forever

19
00:01:30.132 --> 00:01:31.967
♪In the clouds
There she goes♪

20
00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:34.094
♪Butterflies
Let them flow♪

21
00:01:34.177 --> 00:01:36.054
♪'Nother girl falls in love ♪

22
00:01:36.138 --> 00:01:38.140
♪Another girl leaves the club♪

23
00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:44.605
♪I've lost all my senses

24
00:01:44.688 --> 00:01:46.898
♪La-la-la-la
La-la-la-la-la...♪

25
00:01:46.982 --> 00:01:49.109
[applause]

26
00:01:50.027 --> 00:01:51.111
Yeah!

27
00:01:52.195 --> 00:01:53.572
Ah! [chuckles]

28
00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:00.621
What?

29
00:02:00.704 --> 00:02:02.664
Two years in a row. Well done.

30
00:02:02.748 --> 00:02:05.792
Mmm. Yes. thank you. Alert the media.

31
00:02:05.876 --> 00:02:07.252
-Oh. wait. That's us.
-[chuckles]

32
00:02:07.336 --> 00:02:08.670
[announcer] And now we've arrived

33
00:02:08.754 --> 00:02:10.172
at the Golden Keyboard.

34
00:02:10.255 --> 00:02:12.299
for the story or series that constitutes

35
00:02:12.382 --> 00:02:15.552
outstanding enterprise
or investigative reporting.

36
00:02:15.636 --> 00:02:19.097
Our first nominee.
from the New York Vanguard is

37
00:02:19.181 --> 00:02:20.265
Andy Sachs.

38
00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:24.061
for "Hearts of the City:
Stories of Resilience."

39
00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:26.438
Our second nominee.
from the Gotham Sentinel

40
00:02:26.521 --> 00:02:28.690
is George Ali for "The Subway Tapes."

41
00:02:28.774 --> 00:02:30.609
From the Borough Report.

42
00:02:30.692 --> 00:02:33.445
Yvonne Simpson for. "The Cost
Of Medical Care is Terminal."

43
00:02:34.655 --> 00:02:36.281
From the New York Metro Constitution.

44
00:02:36.365 --> 00:02:39.034
Daniel Goldbaum.
for "Fighting Fire with Fire."

45
00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:40.118
It's everybody.

46
00:02:43.038 --> 00:02:44.581
[announcer] And the winner is

47
00:02:44.665 --> 00:02:45.666
Andy Sachs.

48
00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:49.252
[cheering]

49
00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:51.838
Go.

50
00:02:52.422 --> 00:02:53.423
Go.

51
00:02:53.507 --> 00:02:54.508
[attendee] It's great!

52
00:02:54.591 --> 00:02:55.717
That's right. Andy!

53
00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:05.602
[sighs]

54
00:03:06.311 --> 00:03:08.897
Uh... [breathing shakily]

55
00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.274
Thank you for this award. Um...

56
00:03:11.358 --> 00:03:14.152
Sorry. I know I look. uh...
[inhales deeply]

57
00:03:14.236 --> 00:03:17.572
...shocked sad. instead of shocked happy.

58
00:03:17.656 --> 00:03:20.158
and. uh... [stammers]

59
00:03:20.242 --> 00:03:23.161
...that's because I...
I just... [inhales sharply]

60
00:03:23.245 --> 00:03:25.414
I and that whole table

61
00:03:25.497 --> 00:03:29.584
full of talented.
award-winning professionals

62
00:03:29.668 --> 00:03:31.712
from my paper. the Vanguard.

63
00:03:33.088 --> 00:03:34.089
just got fired...

64
00:03:34.172 --> 00:03:35.507
-What?
-[gasping]

65
00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:36.967
-...by text.
-[gasping]

66
00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:43.890
We understand journalism is changing.

67
00:03:43.974 --> 00:03:45.892
but it's still devastating

68
00:03:45.976 --> 00:03:48.228
when something like this happens to you.

69
00:03:48.311 --> 00:03:51.565
Turns out our parent company is taking

70
00:03:51.648 --> 00:03:54.609
a 500-million-dollar write-down.

71
00:03:54.693 --> 00:03:57.362
so we're... [exhales deeply]

72
00:03:57.446 --> 00:03:59.906
...technical term. toast.

73
00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:02.617
[ Ledisi "Daydreaming" playing]

74
00:04:02.701 --> 00:04:05.620
[friend] I can't believe
they just fired everyone.

75
00:04:05.704 --> 00:04:07.748
At least it's a good picture of you.

76
00:04:07.831 --> 00:04:11.251
I just feel so terrible
for everyone at the paper.

77
00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:13.336
John's wife's about to have
their second baby.

78
00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.547
And Allison just bought a house.

79
00:04:16.423 --> 00:04:17.716
Also. it's so unfair.

80
00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:19.593
The CEO of the company that owns the paper

81
00:04:19.676 --> 00:04:21.803
just took home 11 million last year.

82
00:04:21.887 --> 00:04:23.305
Make that make sense.

83
00:04:23.388 --> 00:04:26.892
I can't. But you are gonna be okay.

84
00:04:26.975 --> 00:04:27.976
I don't know. Lil.

85
00:04:28.059 --> 00:04:29.895
Everyone I know is going through this.

86
00:04:29.978 --> 00:04:32.481
Layoffs. downsizing. consolidation.

87
00:04:32.564 --> 00:04:34.441
It's just brutal.

88
00:04:34.524 --> 00:04:36.526
[clicks tongue] Anyway...

89
00:04:37.736 --> 00:04:38.862
I know that I'm lucky.

90
00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:40.781
I know that a lot of people
have it worse than me.

91
00:04:40.864 --> 00:04:42.824
Most people have it worse
than me. It's just...

92
00:04:42.908 --> 00:04:44.075
I'll be fine. I'll be fine.

93
00:04:44.159 --> 00:04:46.036
It is so unfair.

94
00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:49.206
You've worked your ass off
for a whole two decades.

95
00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:50.707
all across the country and the world.

96
00:04:50.791 --> 00:04:52.626
and you never took the easy paycheck.

97
00:04:52.709 --> 00:04:54.961
-Never slept with a colleague.
-Mmm?

98
00:04:55.045 --> 00:04:57.672
-I mean. one.
-Mmm.

99
00:04:57.756 --> 00:04:58.965
Two.

100
00:04:59.049 --> 00:05:00.217
Anyway. the point is.

101
00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.219
is that I never slept with
anyone who could promote me.

102
00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:04.679
just the hot powerless ones.

103
00:05:04.763 --> 00:05:07.182
Are you sure you don't wanna

104
00:05:07.265 --> 00:05:09.851
come and work for me at the gallery?

105
00:05:09.935 --> 00:05:11.770
I need someone who can write decent copy.

106
00:05:11.853 --> 00:05:13.104
And you need a job.

107
00:05:13.188 --> 00:05:14.564
So sweet.

108
00:05:14.648 --> 00:05:16.441
No. thank you. Not yet.

109
00:05:16.525 --> 00:05:18.318
[Miley Cyrus "Walk of Fame" playing]

110
00:05:18.401 --> 00:05:19.528
[horn honks]

111
00:05:20.237 --> 00:05:21.571
So what do I tell her?

112
00:05:22.948 --> 00:05:26.326
Okay. All right. [sighs] Hold on.

113
00:05:26.409 --> 00:05:28.745
You're sure the story
is not gonna break tonight?

114
00:05:28.829 --> 00:05:31.206
PR says we have a day or two.

115
00:05:31.289 --> 00:05:33.250
Well. that's something.

116
00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:35.210
I won't tell her until tomorrow then.

117
00:05:35.293 --> 00:05:37.420
No point in spoiling the evening.

118
00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:41.508
[clamoring]

119
00:05:41.591 --> 00:05:45.846
[singer vocalizing]

120
00:05:45.929 --> 00:05:48.348
♪ Every day ♪

121
00:05:48.431 --> 00:05:51.393
♪Every night it's all the same ♪

122
00:05:54.229 --> 00:05:58.233
♪Hungry cries are callin' out my name ♪

123
00:06:00.735 --> 00:06:02.654
Walk away♪

124
00:06:05.407 --> 00:06:06.449
♪Yeah♪

125
00:06:09.452 --> 00:06:12.914
♪Every time I walk
It's a walk of fame♪

126
00:06:12.998 --> 00:06:16.501
♪ And the cars are bright
And lightin' up my face ♪

127
00:06:18.378 --> 00:06:20.589
-[paparazza 1] It's Miranda.
-[paparazza 2] Is it Miranda?

128
00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:24.175
-Miranda!
-Miranda!

129
00:06:24.259 --> 00:06:27.512
Ooh yeah
It's a walk of fame♪

130
00:06:27.596 --> 00:06:31.725
And through the tears
I can see it so clear♪

131
00:06:32.517 --> 00:06:33.727
Miranda!

132
00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:35.186
Miranda!

133
00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:36.229
♪...walk away

134
00:06:36.313 --> 00:06:37.898
Yeah. every time I walk

135
00:06:37.981 --> 00:06:39.274
It's a walk of fame♪

136
00:06:39.357 --> 00:06:42.485
Ooh yeah
It's a walk of fame♪

137
00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:45.238
And through the mirror... ת

138
00:06:50.619 --> 00:06:52.287
[journalist] Is Runway responsible?

139
00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.289
-[journalists shouting]
-[gasps]

140
00:06:54.372 --> 00:06:55.498
[Amari] Not tonight.

141
00:06:56.583 --> 00:06:58.960
Hi. I just wanted to tell you that

142
00:06:59.044 --> 00:07:00.795
the story broke online...

143
00:07:00.879 --> 00:07:02.172
[Miranda groans]

144
00:07:02.255 --> 00:07:05.425
...and The Business of Fashion
has already run it.

145
00:07:05.508 --> 00:07:08.136
-[sighs] How bad is it?
-A disaster.

146
00:07:08.219 --> 00:07:10.764
It's gone completely viral.
You want to take a look?

147
00:07:11.973 --> 00:07:13.308
Nigel. can I see that?

148
00:07:13.391 --> 00:07:15.685
-Do I have my glasses on?
-Sorry.

149
00:07:15.769 --> 00:07:18.772
Anyway. you're being blamed
for absolutely everything.

150
00:07:18.855 --> 00:07:21.900
-Oh. God. Irv is gonna flip.
-Yep.

151
00:07:21.983 --> 00:07:23.860
And the timing couldn't be worse.

152
00:07:23.944 --> 00:07:26.529
-I know.
-[chuckles]

153
00:07:26.613 --> 00:07:29.199
[Nigel] Chin up. Let's go face the music.

154
00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:40.585
[Miranda] Oh. it's this appalling company

155
00:07:40.669 --> 00:07:42.379
called SpeedFash.

156
00:07:42.462 --> 00:07:44.839
They lied to us about
their working conditions.

157
00:07:44.923 --> 00:07:46.424
They tricked our reporter

158
00:07:46.508 --> 00:07:47.509
and so now.

159
00:07:47.592 --> 00:07:49.427
we are accused of being complicit in

160
00:07:49.511 --> 00:07:52.305
promoting this perfectly horrible company.

161
00:07:52.389 --> 00:07:54.975
The lengths that some people
will go to to turn a profit.

162
00:07:55.058 --> 00:07:57.268
So now we're the villains du jour.

163
00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:01.231
Served up red. piping hot.

164
00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.109
Well. good for you. I say.

165
00:08:04.192 --> 00:08:06.319
Villains are always the most interesting.

166
00:08:06.403 --> 00:08:09.155
[phone buzzing]

167
00:08:09.239 --> 00:08:10.240
Oh. God. Irv.

168
00:08:11.741 --> 00:08:14.828
Ah. That is my cue
to take the dog out for a walk.

169
00:08:14.911 --> 00:08:17.789
Hey. Gio! Let's get this done.

170
00:08:17.872 --> 00:08:19.874
-Come on. boy. [whistles]
-[whines. barks]

171
00:08:19.958 --> 00:08:20.792
Hello. Irv.

172
00:08:20.875 --> 00:08:22.502
What the hell. Miranda?

173
00:08:22.585 --> 00:08:24.421
Dad. check this out.

174
00:08:24.504 --> 00:08:26.506
We are getting killed online.

175
00:08:26.589 --> 00:08:28.883
I am getting angry emails from ad buyers

176
00:08:28.967 --> 00:08:31.469
-for Tiffany. Fendi. Bvlgari!
-[Miranda] Well. I...

177
00:08:31.553 --> 00:08:33.430
Who's gonna pay for your overpriced book?

178
00:08:33.513 --> 00:08:35.473
I've already been on the phone
to advertisers.

179
00:08:35.557 --> 00:08:37.267
I'm meeting with them in the morning

180
00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:39.936
and I'm going to have
a very direct conversation with Ashley.

181
00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:41.771
Don't. I'll fix it.

182
00:08:41.855 --> 00:08:43.231
This is abysmal timing.

183
00:08:43.314 --> 00:08:46.651
I'm pondering this huge move
for you. And this happens.

184
00:08:47.902 --> 00:08:49.070
[beeping]

185
00:08:51.614 --> 00:08:53.241
[son] Dad. this is bad.

186
00:08:53.324 --> 00:08:55.410
Even the Journal is thrashing Runway.

187
00:08:55.493 --> 00:08:57.537
and all of Elias-Clarke is getting blamed.

188
00:08:57.620 --> 00:08:58.913
Our monkeys. our circus.

189
00:08:58.997 --> 00:09:00.165
I know.

190
00:09:00.248 --> 00:09:03.126
How are we gonna claw back
any shred of credibility?

191
00:09:04.586 --> 00:09:05.587
Check this out.

192
00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:10.341
Because some things
still matter more than money.

193
00:09:10.425 --> 00:09:12.927
Journalism still fucking matters!

194
00:09:13.011 --> 00:09:14.512
[cheering. whistling]

195
00:09:35.867 --> 00:09:36.701
Whoa.

196
00:09:43.833 --> 00:09:45.794
Yo.

197
00:09:48.671 --> 00:09:50.090
[gulps. gasps]

198
00:09:54.511 --> 00:09:56.513
[host 1] Miranda Priestly?

199
00:09:56.596 --> 00:09:58.264
I thought she was smarter than this.

200
00:09:58.348 --> 00:09:59.891
[host 2] At this point.
it's like you're not relevant.

201
00:09:59.974 --> 00:10:01.267
-[host 1] Oh. my God.
-[host 2] She's a relic.

202
00:10:01.351 --> 00:10:02.685
She's dead. She's a dinosaur.

203
00:10:02.769 --> 00:10:04.270
[host 1] Like. old. tired. washed.

204
00:10:04.354 --> 00:10:05.438
I know it.

205
00:10:05.522 --> 00:10:07.148
She watchin' the season finale
of Yellowstone.

206
00:10:07.232 --> 00:10:09.567
-[host 2] No. fully.
-[hosts laughing]

207
00:10:09.651 --> 00:10:10.652
[phone ringing]

208
00:10:13.863 --> 00:10:14.697
Hello?

209
00:10:14.781 --> 00:10:15.865
[Irv] Andy Sachs?

210
00:10:15.949 --> 00:10:16.950
Yes.

211
00:10:17.033 --> 00:10:19.494
Irv Ravitz. Quite a speech you made today.

212
00:10:19.577 --> 00:10:22.664
-Oh. Hi.
-Looks like you need a job.

213
00:10:22.747 --> 00:10:22.872
Post Animal "Setting Sun" playing]

214
00:10:22.872 --> 00:10:22.956
Post Animal "Setting Sun" playing]

215
00:10:22.956 --> 00:10:25.041
Post Animal "Setting Sun" playing]

216
00:10:25.125 --> 00:10:27.168
I can't even believe I'm thinking about

217
00:10:27.252 --> 00:10:29.045
going back to work at that magazine.

218
00:10:29.129 --> 00:10:30.964
-How's the salary?
-[inhales deeply]

219
00:10:31.047 --> 00:10:33.883
Twice what I made at the Vanguard

220
00:10:33.967 --> 00:10:35.677
and Irv promised that

221
00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.221
I would have a real budget to tell stories

222
00:10:38.304 --> 00:10:40.807
and hire real writers
like you guys. so I don't know.

223
00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:42.976
Hey. No one here is judging you.

224
00:10:43.059 --> 00:10:45.895
I am currently editing a memoir

225
00:10:45.979 --> 00:10:48.022
by one of Paris Hilton's Chihuahuas.

226
00:10:48.106 --> 00:10:50.441
a sassy applehead named Chi-town.

227
00:10:50.525 --> 00:10:52.861
Just to back up. you can hire us.

228
00:10:52.944 --> 00:10:55.780
Your complete abandonment
of your principles means a lot.

229
00:10:55.864 --> 00:10:57.657
[chuckling]

230
00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:59.450
Cheers to that. Pay that rent. man.

231
00:10:59.951 --> 00:11:01.119
[chuckles]

232
00:11:01.202 --> 00:11:02.620
You know what you could do?

233
00:11:02.704 --> 00:11:05.707
If you took the job.
you could write a book.

234
00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:09.752
The definitive Miranda Priestly expose.

235
00:11:09.836 --> 00:11:10.879
No. I couldn't do that.

236
00:11:10.962 --> 00:11:13.673
An insider's book about Miranda
could be huge.

237
00:11:13.756 --> 00:11:15.216
-I'm gonna text my boss.
-[Andy] No. No. no. no.

238
00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:16.885
-I'm just curious.
-[Andy] Don't you dare.

239
00:11:16.968 --> 00:11:18.219
I bet you could get 50K for it.

240
00:11:18.303 --> 00:11:20.138
No. no-- Like. really don't do that.
No. I... No. no. no.

241
00:11:20.221 --> 00:11:22.515
Guys. I would never get hired
again. By anyone.

242
00:11:22.599 --> 00:11:23.725
-She has a point.
-[phone dings]

243
00:11:23.808 --> 00:11:25.351
Some whiny girl
complaining about her boss.

244
00:11:25.435 --> 00:11:27.604
Okay. he said a well-written proposal.

245
00:11:27.687 --> 00:11:28.980
and we all know you do write well.

246
00:11:29.063 --> 00:11:30.356
could get you something like 100K.

247
00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:31.399
No.

248
00:11:31.482 --> 00:11:32.442
[friend 2] Yeah. I'm totally in on this.

249
00:11:32.525 --> 00:11:33.610
No. I'm sorry. I'm just not that person.

250
00:11:33.693 --> 00:11:35.945
I'm not. Maybe I can
make something of this job.

251
00:11:36.029 --> 00:11:39.365
Runway does have a history
of publishing great writing.

252
00:11:39.449 --> 00:11:41.409
No. I'm gonna walk in there tomorrow

253
00:11:41.492 --> 00:11:43.244
with a big smile on my face

254
00:11:43.328 --> 00:11:46.706
and I'm going to make something
of this job. Okay?

255
00:11:57.467 --> 00:11:58.760
What?

256
00:11:58.843 --> 00:12:01.888
"Don't worry. I fixed it.
Be ready at 9:00. Irv."

257
00:12:01.971 --> 00:12:05.266
And you-know-who demanded
we be crosstown at 9:15.

258
00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:06.684
so how do we do both?

259
00:12:06.768 --> 00:12:08.353
We don't have time for this.

260
00:12:08.436 --> 00:12:09.771
What does he mean. "I fixed it"?

261
00:12:09.854 --> 00:12:11.981
[sighs] I have no idea.

262
00:12:12.065 --> 00:12:13.650
-Your 9:00 a.m. is here.
-[Miranda] Hmm.

263
00:12:15.652 --> 00:12:16.653
[gasps]

264
00:12:18.529 --> 00:12:19.781
Hello.

265
00:12:20.531 --> 00:12:21.449
Miranda.

266
00:12:21.532 --> 00:12:23.493
[gasps] Nigel.

267
00:12:23.576 --> 00:12:26.454
Look what TJ Maxx dragged in.

268
00:12:26.537 --> 00:12:28.831
-Hello. Six.
-Hello. [chuckles]

269
00:12:30.625 --> 00:12:33.586
Thank you so much for this opportunity.

270
00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:34.921
I... I've got to admit.

271
00:12:35.004 --> 00:12:36.714
I was really surprised when Irv called.

272
00:12:36.798 --> 00:12:39.259
I mean. a lot of time
has gone by. but I'm just...

273
00:12:40.385 --> 00:12:42.345
I'm so happy for the chance to...

274
00:12:42.428 --> 00:12:43.471
Sorry.

275
00:12:43.554 --> 00:12:45.723
Who is this? Do you know her?
Do I know her?

276
00:12:45.807 --> 00:12:49.602
You might remember Andy.
She was one of the Emilys.

277
00:12:49.686 --> 00:12:50.687
One of the what?

278
00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:53.147
-The Emily from... It's...
-I'm Andy Sachs.

279
00:12:53.231 --> 00:12:54.899
Uh-- Uh. the smart fat girl.

280
00:12:55.942 --> 00:12:58.152
I was one of your assistants eons ago.

281
00:12:58.236 --> 00:12:59.445
Maybe Irv didn't fill you in.

282
00:12:59.529 --> 00:13:01.322
And Irv sent you here to...

283
00:13:01.406 --> 00:13:05.410
To run the Features department.

284
00:13:05.493 --> 00:13:08.621
Be the Features editor.
He hired me last night.

285
00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:10.373
He didn't tell you?

286
00:13:12.917 --> 00:13:13.918
Um...

287
00:13:15.712 --> 00:13:20.300
So he said that. um. we need to. um...

288
00:13:20.383 --> 00:13:22.343
I mean. Runway needs to take control

289
00:13:22.427 --> 00:13:23.928
of the narrative of the SpeedFash story.

290
00:13:24.012 --> 00:13:25.972
and-and-and restore credibility.

291
00:13:26.055 --> 00:13:30.143
and he thought someone with my
expertise would be a good idea.

292
00:13:30.226 --> 00:13:34.272
but I thought that for sure
you had approved and were excited.

293
00:13:34.355 --> 00:13:35.773
And that's why I came in here all like...

294
00:13:35.857 --> 00:13:36.941
]singsongy] A-oh! ת

295
00:13:41.529 --> 00:13:43.114
-Amari.
-Yes.

296
00:13:43.197 --> 00:13:45.241
Get Ashley on the phone. please.

297
00:13:46.993 --> 00:13:48.077
[rings. clicks]

298
00:13:48.161 --> 00:13:49.787
[Ashley] Hi. Good morning. Miranda.

299
00:13:49.871 --> 00:13:51.414
Yes. Ashley. Flag on the play.

300
00:13:51.497 --> 00:13:54.250
We don't need you this morning or ever.

301
00:13:54.334 --> 00:13:55.960
so pack up your things.

302
00:13:56.044 --> 00:13:58.588
And HR will be in to see you shortly.

303
00:13:58.671 --> 00:13:59.505
Uh...

304
00:13:59.589 --> 00:14:00.590
Happy?

305
00:14:01.716 --> 00:14:05.553
You just cost Cornell
summa cum laude her job.

306
00:14:05.636 --> 00:14:08.556
First in her family
to go to college. I believe.

307
00:14:09.599 --> 00:14:11.017
Anyway. come along.

308
00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:12.602
[Nigel] Come on.

309
00:14:24.906 --> 00:14:25.907
[Andy] Uh...

310
00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:31.871
[grunts]

311
00:14:34.332 --> 00:14:37.126
-You should get that looked at.
-Hmm?

312
00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:39.295
The injury. or condition. or whatever.

313
00:14:39.379 --> 00:14:41.297
that causes you to trudge.

314
00:14:42.006 --> 00:14:43.007
[Miranda sighs]

315
00:14:43.091 --> 00:14:45.635
So. um. I was thinking that...

316
00:14:45.718 --> 00:14:48.012
[clears throat]
...when we get back to the offices.

317
00:14:48.096 --> 00:14:49.097
we could sit down

318
00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:51.474
and go through some of your priorities.

319
00:14:51.557 --> 00:14:53.059
I could share some of my insights

320
00:14:53.142 --> 00:14:54.727
into the editorial direction
of the magazine.

321
00:14:54.811 --> 00:14:56.104
First. though.

322
00:14:56.187 --> 00:14:59.440
we need to appease
an important advertiser.

323
00:14:59.524 --> 00:15:01.150
-I-Is that where we're going?
-[Nigel] Yes.

324
00:15:01.234 --> 00:15:03.027
They're furious about SpeedFash.

325
00:15:03.111 --> 00:15:06.239
[sighs] God knows what they're
going to extract from me.

326
00:15:06.322 --> 00:15:08.825
Free ad space. targeted editorial.

327
00:15:08.908 --> 00:15:10.785
A cornea.

328
00:15:10.868 --> 00:15:13.204
Luckily. though. I'm not worried

329
00:15:13.287 --> 00:15:16.999
'cause I have you flown in from on high

330
00:15:17.083 --> 00:15:20.586
to help redirect
this century-old institution

331
00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:23.423
with the benefit of
your infinite wisdom and what?

332
00:15:23.506 --> 00:15:25.633
Oh. Expertise.

333
00:15:26.342 --> 00:15:27.593
Yeah.

334
00:15:28.886 --> 00:15:33.015
Dior constitutes
16% of our designer spend.

335
00:15:33.099 --> 00:15:35.268
And are leading sponsors
of all our special events.

336
00:15:35.351 --> 00:15:36.352
which ain't cheap.

337
00:15:36.436 --> 00:15:38.312
Okay. so we fix this. we got this.

338
00:15:38.396 --> 00:15:39.522
Who do we have to talk to?

339
00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:47.989
Am I having a hallucination?

340
00:15:48.614 --> 00:15:49.657
Emily. hi.

341
00:15:50.533 --> 00:15:51.993
Oh. wow.

342
00:15:53.161 --> 00:15:55.079
You also know her?

343
00:15:55.163 --> 00:15:57.790
Well. we were at Runway
at the same time. Miranda.

344
00:15:57.874 --> 00:16:00.501
Really? Where was I?

345
00:16:02.503 --> 00:16:05.756
Right. Um. shall we?

346
00:16:08.301 --> 00:16:09.927
Ms. Charlton. you're glowing.

347
00:16:10.011 --> 00:16:11.762
It seems retail still suits you.

348
00:16:11.846 --> 00:16:14.515
Ah. Nigel. you're hanging in there
by your fingernails.

349
00:16:14.599 --> 00:16:16.392
Remember when magazines were a thing?

350
00:16:16.476 --> 00:16:19.604
All right. Come on in.
everybody. Take a seat.

351
00:16:20.688 --> 00:16:22.940
-Lots to discuss.
-[Nigel] Mm-hmm.

352
00:16:23.941 --> 00:16:26.027
Um... Where would you like to start?

353
00:16:26.777 --> 00:16:28.196
[sighs]

354
00:16:28.279 --> 00:16:31.991
Okay. So. um. I'm the new
Features editor at Runway.

355
00:16:32.074 --> 00:16:33.659
No. you're not.

356
00:16:35.286 --> 00:16:36.579
Are you serious?

357
00:16:36.662 --> 00:16:39.248
Wow. Wonders never cease.

358
00:16:39.332 --> 00:16:41.584
No. I'm actually a journalist now.

359
00:16:41.667 --> 00:16:45.171
Um. I've been published in...
It doesn't matter.

360
00:16:45.254 --> 00:16:47.423
Anyway. we are all well aware
that running that story

361
00:16:47.507 --> 00:16:49.300
was a mistake and are taking
immediate steps--

362
00:16:49.383 --> 00:16:51.010
I cannot actually get over this.

363
00:16:51.886 --> 00:16:53.429
It's really remarkable.

364
00:16:53.513 --> 00:16:56.974
A senior editor at Runway. You.

365
00:16:57.058 --> 00:16:58.226
Yep.

366
00:16:58.309 --> 00:17:00.686
-We're all so thrilled.
-Mm-hmm.

367
00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:02.438
You know what's funny. is you've changed.

368
00:17:02.522 --> 00:17:04.065
You have. You're much more confident.

369
00:17:04.148 --> 00:17:05.233
Oh.

370
00:17:05.316 --> 00:17:06.984
[Emily] Kept those eyebrows
though. didn't you?

371
00:17:07.068 --> 00:17:10.071
Um. right. Miranda. [sighs] How are you?

372
00:17:10.154 --> 00:17:11.447
I mean. what a shambles.

373
00:17:11.531 --> 00:17:14.325
I've had to unruffle so many feathers
because as you know.

374
00:17:14.408 --> 00:17:17.078
our association with Runway and with you

375
00:17:17.161 --> 00:17:20.248
is predicated on your sterling reputation.

376
00:17:20.331 --> 00:17:21.999
[scoffs] Momentary lapse.

377
00:17:22.083 --> 00:17:24.627
And we're doing everything we can
to make it right with you.

378
00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:26.504
Good. 'cause I was
quite shocked reading it.

379
00:17:26.587 --> 00:17:28.214
Though. I'm sure. as you can appreciate.

380
00:17:28.297 --> 00:17:30.258
it's also important
that we have editorial freedom.

381
00:17:30.341 --> 00:17:32.134
We have to maintain
our journalistic integrity.

382
00:17:32.218 --> 00:17:34.178
Ooh. Integrity. I see.

383
00:17:34.262 --> 00:17:37.056
La-di-da. Andy. Very high-minded of you.

384
00:17:37.139 --> 00:17:39.100
Well. please help yourself
to standards galore.

385
00:17:39.183 --> 00:17:41.060
but if there are no advertisers.

386
00:17:41.143 --> 00:17:42.520
there's no Runway.

387
00:17:42.603 --> 00:17:43.854
No us. no you.

388
00:17:43.938 --> 00:17:45.565
And we understand that completely.

389
00:17:45.648 --> 00:17:46.774
Oh. good.

390
00:17:46.857 --> 00:17:50.194
I think as a consequence
of your lapse in judgment.

391
00:17:50.278 --> 00:17:51.487
there's just a few things we'd quite like

392
00:17:51.571 --> 00:17:53.281
just to smooth it all over.

393
00:17:53.364 --> 00:17:56.909
We are thinking at least
three pages of advertising credit

394
00:17:56.993 --> 00:18:00.705
and a feature on the opening
of our new flagship.

395
00:18:00.788 --> 00:18:01.998
-Six pages.
-Three.

396
00:18:02.081 --> 00:18:04.041
-No. Five.
-Four.

397
00:18:04.125 --> 00:18:06.294
I'm sorry. Are we at a fish market. Nigel?

398
00:18:06.377 --> 00:18:09.380
It's five. with brand mentions
in all captions.

399
00:18:10.840 --> 00:18:12.049
Miranda?

400
00:18:12.133 --> 00:18:15.177
Absolutely. We'll get
right on that immediately.

401
00:18:15.261 --> 00:18:16.178
[Emily] Great.

402
00:18:22.393 --> 00:18:24.979
I'm confused. You just
let them tell you what to do?

403
00:18:25.605 --> 00:18:26.731
Because if I'm here

404
00:18:26.814 --> 00:18:28.482
to restore your credibility. and I...

405
00:18:28.566 --> 00:18:31.402
Did you not hear her? No them. no us.

406
00:18:31.485 --> 00:18:33.112
We need our advertisers. Andrea.

407
00:18:33.195 --> 00:18:34.947
The September issue is already so thin

408
00:18:35.031 --> 00:18:36.032
you could floss with it.

409
00:18:36.115 --> 00:18:37.825
Yeah. advertisers are important.

410
00:18:37.908 --> 00:18:39.910
I know that. I'm not brand-new. but...

411
00:18:39.994 --> 00:18:41.203
Let's be clear.

412
00:18:42.455 --> 00:18:44.874
You did not earn this job.
I did not hire you.

413
00:18:44.957 --> 00:18:48.336
You are a CEO's latest whim.

414
00:18:48.419 --> 00:18:51.672
And all I need to do is to
bide my time until you fail.

415
00:18:53.382 --> 00:18:55.468
And you will. Fail.

416
00:18:58.971 --> 00:19:00.473
Why don't you take the train. dear?

417
00:19:26.082 --> 00:19:27.958
You got this. You got this.

418
00:19:28.042 --> 00:19:29.710
You... did it.

419
00:19:30.795 --> 00:19:31.796
Okay.

420
00:19:31.879 --> 00:19:33.130
[sighs]

421
00:19:34.382 --> 00:19:35.383
[keys clatter]

422
00:19:37.968 --> 00:19:39.470
They're just eyebrows.

423
00:19:40.471 --> 00:19:42.306
[♪ upbeat music playing]

424
00:19:48.854 --> 00:19:50.106
[elevator bell dings]

425
00:19:50.189 --> 00:19:51.691
[staff chattering]

426
00:20:02.535 --> 00:20:03.577
-Hi!
-Hi.

427
00:20:03.661 --> 00:20:05.121
We weren't properly introduced yesterday.

428
00:20:05.204 --> 00:20:06.205
I'm Andy Sachs.

429
00:20:06.288 --> 00:20:07.748
Charlie. I'm the second assistant.

430
00:20:07.832 --> 00:20:08.666
Hi. Charlie!

431
00:20:08.749 --> 00:20:09.917
Hey. Everybody just calls me

432
00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:11.210
"Charlie the Chair." if you want.

433
00:20:11.293 --> 00:20:13.212
'cause Amari won't let me leave my desk.

434
00:20:13.295 --> 00:20:14.755
Oh. no. it's okay. [chuckles]

435
00:20:14.839 --> 00:20:15.923
A million girls would kill for this job.

436
00:20:16.006 --> 00:20:17.717
Heard that. [chuckles]

437
00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:19.593
-Is Miranda in?
-There you are.

438
00:20:19.677 --> 00:20:22.096
There you are. I am on time.

439
00:20:22.179 --> 00:20:23.431
So... [scoffs]

440
00:20:23.514 --> 00:20:25.433
...you're the kind of person
who thinks on time is on time.

441
00:20:25.516 --> 00:20:26.851
Okay. got it.

442
00:20:26.934 --> 00:20:28.185
All right. come on.
I'll show you to your office.

443
00:20:28.269 --> 00:20:31.313
Amari. I need to pee.
Please. I had a venti.

444
00:20:31.397 --> 00:20:32.440
Oh.

445
00:20:32.523 --> 00:20:33.733
Was it worth it?

446
00:20:33.816 --> 00:20:34.942
-No.
-No.

447
00:20:35.025 --> 00:20:36.986
Actually. I should probably
talk to Miranda first.

448
00:20:37.069 --> 00:20:39.196
Oh. I'll take you to your office.

449
00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:41.699
-Good luck. Charlie.
-Thanks.

450
00:20:41.782 --> 00:20:43.743
So. I used to have your job.

451
00:20:43.826 --> 00:20:45.661
I heard. Such a remarkable fact.

452
00:20:45.745 --> 00:20:48.789
Yeah. back in 2006.

453
00:20:48.873 --> 00:20:50.624
[chuckles] Got to go
to Paris Fashion Week.

454
00:20:50.708 --> 00:20:52.251
Wore a bunch of pieces
from the Chanel collection

455
00:20:52.334 --> 00:20:53.294
that year.

456
00:20:53.377 --> 00:20:55.421
Oh. wait. The one with the pageboy hats?

457
00:20:55.504 --> 00:20:57.465
And the thigh-high boots. Yeah!

458
00:20:57.548 --> 00:20:58.966
Love. Do you still have all of it?

459
00:20:59.049 --> 00:21:00.968
-No. I gave it away.
-What?

460
00:21:01.051 --> 00:21:02.887
Would've been a bit much for a newsroom.

461
00:21:05.181 --> 00:21:06.182
This is you.

462
00:21:06.265 --> 00:21:07.683
Oh. Okay.

463
00:21:14.482 --> 00:21:15.691
And this was Ashley's office?

464
00:21:15.775 --> 00:21:18.277
No. Miranda wanted you to have
this one. for some reason.

465
00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:19.528
For some reason. Right.

466
00:21:20.654 --> 00:21:22.156
Who gives away Chanel?

467
00:21:32.583 --> 00:21:34.502
-I stole these for you.
-Hey.

468
00:21:34.585 --> 00:21:36.003
-All from this season.
-[gasps]

469
00:21:36.086 --> 00:21:38.547
But lie about where you got them.

470
00:21:38.631 --> 00:21:41.675
There's more where that came from.
but you can't be greedy.

471
00:21:41.759 --> 00:21:44.094
-Cafeteria?
-Great. I'm all done.

472
00:21:44.178 --> 00:21:45.179
Come on.

473
00:21:45.805 --> 00:21:47.139
Hmm.

474
00:21:47.223 --> 00:21:50.267
I don't mind that blazer. Is it...

475
00:21:50.351 --> 00:21:51.977
-Margiela.
-Huh.

476
00:21:52.061 --> 00:21:54.730
Thrifted it in Provo. Eleven bucks.

477
00:21:54.814 --> 00:21:56.565
Learned a thing or two
the last time I was here. babe.

478
00:21:56.649 --> 00:21:58.901
Hmm! Not bad. babe.

479
00:21:58.984 --> 00:22:00.528
[Jamiroquai "Diskokid" playing]

480
00:22:00.611 --> 00:22:02.279
Ah. fate.

481
00:22:02.363 --> 00:22:04.698
The winding road
that brings us back to me.

482
00:22:04.782 --> 00:22:07.117
watching you ladle goo into a bowl.

483
00:22:07.201 --> 00:22:08.410
[chuckles]

484
00:22:09.453 --> 00:22:10.871
It's so good to see you. Nigel.

485
00:22:10.955 --> 00:22:12.122
Yeah.

486
00:22:12.206 --> 00:22:14.375
Miranda is very lucky
you're still at the magazine.

487
00:22:15.376 --> 00:22:16.502
What magazine?

488
00:22:16.585 --> 00:22:19.046
You must know that Runway
stopped being a magazine

489
00:22:19.129 --> 00:22:20.464
many years ago.

490
00:22:20.548 --> 00:22:23.884
I mean. we still have a book.
but practically nobody buys it.

491
00:22:23.968 --> 00:22:26.720
Nope. We are digital. We are downloadable.

492
00:22:26.804 --> 00:22:28.889
We are streamable. We're in the ether.

493
00:22:30.224 --> 00:22:31.308
And the budgets?

494
00:22:31.392 --> 00:22:34.520
I used to get four weeks
in Africa with Avedon

495
00:22:34.603 --> 00:22:35.813
to do a glossy spread.

496
00:22:35.896 --> 00:22:39.650
and now I'm lucky if I get
two days at Milk Studios

497
00:22:39.733 --> 00:22:44.488
to shoot content that people
scroll past whilst they pee.

498
00:22:47.449 --> 00:22:48.284
[groans]

499
00:22:48.367 --> 00:22:50.619
-So...
-Yeah?

500
00:22:50.703 --> 00:22:55.416
Is it just me.
or has Miranda been extra spicy?

501
00:22:55.499 --> 00:22:57.251
You see her outside of Dior?

502
00:22:57.334 --> 00:22:59.295
I thought her head was going to
spin completely around.

503
00:22:59.378 --> 00:23:02.464
Well. she's on edge because Irv
is about to promote her

504
00:23:02.548 --> 00:23:04.008
to Global Head of Content.

505
00:23:04.091 --> 00:23:05.634
-No way. That's a huge job.
-Yeah.

506
00:23:05.718 --> 00:23:09.763
But the problem is. the SpeedFash debacle
might derail the whole thing.

507
00:23:09.847 --> 00:23:13.100
What you're saying is. she needs me.

508
00:23:13.183 --> 00:23:14.685
[chuckles]

509
00:23:18.856 --> 00:23:20.774
-Oh. you're serious.
-[chokes]

510
00:23:20.858 --> 00:23:21.692
Sorry.

511
00:23:21.775 --> 00:23:22.610
-Oh. really.
-Yeah.

512
00:23:22.693 --> 00:23:23.777
I'm going up to the art department.

513
00:23:23.861 --> 00:23:25.529
-Oh. you like that.
-[laughing] Yeah. very much.

514
00:23:25.613 --> 00:23:26.697
-Are you Andy?
-Yes.

515
00:23:26.780 --> 00:23:28.490
Hi. I'm Jin Chao. I'm your new assistant.

516
00:23:28.574 --> 00:23:29.575
-Cool! I was wondering...
-Hi.

517
00:23:29.658 --> 00:23:31.076
...about that. Nice to meet you.

518
00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:33.704
I was an intern this morning.
but. uh. when a desk opens up.

519
00:23:33.787 --> 00:23:36.206
the interns get a chance
to interview. and guess what?

520
00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:37.666
Nobody wanted to work in your department

521
00:23:37.750 --> 00:23:39.001
'cause it's not actual fashion.

522
00:23:39.084 --> 00:23:40.836
so I just. like. got it. Isn't that cool?

523
00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:42.046
Very.

524
00:23:42.129 --> 00:23:44.548
-You don't want me.
-[stammers] I didn't say that.

525
00:23:44.632 --> 00:23:46.342
If you don't want me.
you can interview someone else.

526
00:23:46.425 --> 00:23:47.635
-That's totally fine.
-I don't--

527
00:23:47.718 --> 00:23:49.303
I did go to Yale. 3.86 GPA.

528
00:23:49.386 --> 00:23:50.596
lead soprano of the Whiffenpoofs.

529
00:23:50.679 --> 00:23:53.057
and my ACT score was 36
on the very first try. but...

530
00:23:53.140 --> 00:23:54.850
No. you seem great. I'm happy to have you.

531
00:23:54.934 --> 00:23:56.268
-Great. Okay. Nice.
-Okay. great. Okay.

532
00:23:56.352 --> 00:23:58.020
Um. so. listen. I'm going
to be working late tonight.

533
00:23:58.103 --> 00:24:00.522
so I'm just going to need
a couple things. First. I j--

534
00:24:01.815 --> 00:24:04.777
Is Miranda hanging up her own coat?

535
00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:07.655
Oh. yeah. I heard there were
some HR complaints.

536
00:24:07.738 --> 00:24:11.033
Apparently. she used to
just throw her coat at people.

537
00:24:11.116 --> 00:24:12.076
Huh.

538
00:24:12.159 --> 00:24:13.160
[closet door slams]

539
00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:17.998
[exhales deeply]

540
00:24:21.377 --> 00:24:23.671
Maybe some things have changed.

541
00:24:23.754 --> 00:24:25.798
Ah. Anyway. yes. working late tonight.

542
00:24:25.881 --> 00:24:27.383
[chattering]

543
00:24:30.052 --> 00:24:32.221
[Andy muttering indistinctly]

544
00:24:42.147 --> 00:24:44.149
You're not still waiting for
the book. are you?

545
00:24:44.233 --> 00:24:46.193
Oh. yeah. She likes hard copies.

546
00:24:46.276 --> 00:24:47.611
[sighs] Still?

547
00:24:47.695 --> 00:24:48.904
[chuckles] Mm-hmm.

548
00:24:48.988 --> 00:24:51.115
Um. can I give you something
to bring over to her?

549
00:24:51.198 --> 00:24:52.491
'Course. I'll hoof it over.

550
00:24:52.574 --> 00:24:53.617
-Thank you.
-Yeah.

551
00:24:53.701 --> 00:24:55.369
Oh. When you go to her house.

552
00:24:55.452 --> 00:24:57.538
no matter what anyone tells you.

553
00:24:57.621 --> 00:24:59.581
don't go up the stairs. ever.

554
00:24:59.665 --> 00:25:00.541
[chuckles]

555
00:25:00.624 --> 00:25:01.750
Who'd be stupid enough to do that?

556
00:25:01.834 --> 00:25:02.835
No one!

557
00:25:08.924 --> 00:25:10.134
[phone buzzes]

558
00:25:19.351 --> 00:25:20.352
[chuckles]

559
00:25:27.943 --> 00:25:29.486
Vanessa Friedman in The Times

560
00:25:29.570 --> 00:25:32.865
says that Andy's story
is a "bracing mea culpa."

561
00:25:32.948 --> 00:25:35.868
Yeah. but did anyone
besides culture writers

562
00:25:35.951 --> 00:25:37.536
actually click on the story?

563
00:25:37.619 --> 00:25:41.373
Not really. but optics-wise.
saved our bacon. right?

564
00:25:41.457 --> 00:25:42.958
We'll see. Jury's still out.

565
00:25:45.919 --> 00:25:47.588
[♪ upbeat music playing]

566
00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:54.386
-Good morning.
-Hi.

567
00:26:00.225 --> 00:26:01.685
Nigel in?

568
00:26:01.769 --> 00:26:03.771
No. Nigel's not in.

569
00:26:09.943 --> 00:26:10.944
-I read your piece.
-Hmm?

570
00:26:11.028 --> 00:26:12.321
I read your piece. It was really good.

571
00:26:12.404 --> 00:26:14.073
Oh. thank you. [chuckles]

572
00:26:15.074 --> 00:26:17.201
Oh. See. that was a cool profile.

573
00:26:17.284 --> 00:26:19.411
Oh. yeah. The Barnes piece.

574
00:26:19.495 --> 00:26:22.164
Obviously. it was from before their split.

575
00:26:22.247 --> 00:26:23.165
Yeah.

576
00:26:23.248 --> 00:26:25.000
-I love Sasha Barnes.
-I know.

577
00:26:25.084 --> 00:26:26.335
[Andy] I've tried to get
an interview with her.

578
00:26:26.418 --> 00:26:28.378
like. a million times
but I've always struck out.

579
00:26:28.462 --> 00:26:30.464
-Oh. you're not the only one.
-Really?

580
00:26:30.547 --> 00:26:31.715
Miranda would kill.

581
00:26:31.799 --> 00:26:33.383
Now that their divorce has made Sasha

582
00:26:33.467 --> 00:26:34.843
one of the richest women in the world.

583
00:26:34.927 --> 00:26:36.428
Interesting.

584
00:26:36.512 --> 00:26:37.846
I feel like I could write a book about

585
00:26:37.930 --> 00:26:40.265
just his before and after.
You know what I mean?

586
00:26:40.349 --> 00:26:42.059
The original Benji Barnes was.
like. that slope-shouldered

587
00:26:42.142 --> 00:26:45.395
nerd who's like. never seen
the inside of a weight room.

588
00:26:45.479 --> 00:26:47.189
Lately... There was a picture
of him the other day.

589
00:26:47.272 --> 00:26:51.652
A few steroids.
a smidge of Sculptra. some Ozempic

590
00:26:51.735 --> 00:26:53.195
-and voila.
-He's a modern miracle.

591
00:26:53.278 --> 00:26:54.571
It's a modern miracle.

592
00:26:54.655 --> 00:26:55.864
Gotta love a glow up.

593
00:26:55.948 --> 00:26:56.949
It's compelling.

594
00:26:58.117 --> 00:27:00.536
Okay. well-- Ooh. Hi.

595
00:27:07.126 --> 00:27:09.878
We took some vintage Muglers
and Westwoods.

596
00:27:09.962 --> 00:27:12.673
and we shot them downtown
in the Washington Mews.

597
00:27:12.756 --> 00:27:14.508
And you were there

598
00:27:14.591 --> 00:27:16.426
when these images were taken?

599
00:27:17.386 --> 00:27:18.595
Uh...

600
00:27:18.679 --> 00:27:20.472
Yes. I-I was.

601
00:27:20.556 --> 00:27:23.267
So the intention was dull and listless.

602
00:27:23.350 --> 00:27:26.270
I wouldn't say that was.
um. the goal. [titters]

603
00:27:26.353 --> 00:27:27.938
And the models were encouraged

604
00:27:28.021 --> 00:27:31.608
to mill around like starving goats

605
00:27:31.692 --> 00:27:35.445
in the parking lot of
a methadone clinic in New Jersey.

606
00:27:35.529 --> 00:27:36.488
[Amari clears throat]

607
00:27:36.572 --> 00:27:37.948
-What?
-Can't say that.

608
00:27:38.031 --> 00:27:39.324
What am I not allowed to say?

609
00:27:39.408 --> 00:27:40.659
-Methadone?
-[Amari] Mmm.

610
00:27:40.742 --> 00:27:43.704
New Jersey? [chuckles] Anyway...

611
00:27:43.787 --> 00:27:45.914
This was our first time
using this photographer.

612
00:27:45.998 --> 00:27:47.124
so we can do a reshoot.

613
00:27:47.207 --> 00:27:49.793
Just. you know. fix it.

614
00:27:49.877 --> 00:27:54.423
So. who else? Marta?
What do you have cooking?

615
00:27:54.506 --> 00:27:56.884
We're seeing a lot of gorpcore
for this year's resort.

616
00:27:56.967 --> 00:28:01.138
so I was thinking of doing
an interactive piece on the app

617
00:28:01.221 --> 00:28:02.890
where you choose a National Park

618
00:28:02.973 --> 00:28:06.185
and then you choose
a hiking shoe and a bum bag.

619
00:28:06.268 --> 00:28:08.061
A bum bag!

620
00:28:08.145 --> 00:28:10.856
May my suicide be brief and painless.

621
00:28:10.939 --> 00:28:11.815
[Amari clears throat]

622
00:28:11.899 --> 00:28:13.233
-No. no.
-What?

623
00:28:13.317 --> 00:28:14.484
What do you mean. "No. no."

624
00:28:14.568 --> 00:28:18.614
I... I'm not talking about
killing other people. Yet.

625
00:28:20.407 --> 00:28:23.994
Okay. Fine. Uh. who else?

626
00:28:24.077 --> 00:28:25.204
Ilana. what do you have?

627
00:28:25.287 --> 00:28:28.999
The re-report of the SpeedFash
story went over well.

628
00:28:29.082 --> 00:28:32.544
Media critics responded to our candor

629
00:28:32.628 --> 00:28:34.838
and to us taking responsibility.

630
00:28:34.922 --> 00:28:38.342
Right. But did anyone read the piece?

631
00:28:38.425 --> 00:28:39.468
I don't know.

632
00:28:39.551 --> 00:28:41.345
Nigel. how are the metrics on that one?

633
00:28:41.428 --> 00:28:44.306
-[Nigel] Mm-mmm.
-Hmm-mmm.

634
00:28:44.389 --> 00:28:49.770
See. you are here to write and edit
features that people read.

635
00:28:49.853 --> 00:28:53.190
And when that happens. yes.
you may interrupt a meeting.

636
00:28:53.941 --> 00:28:56.693
But until then... [winces]

637
00:28:57.694 --> 00:28:58.946
Mm-mmm.

638
00:29:00.322 --> 00:29:04.243
Let's talk about rodeo bow ties.

639
00:29:04.326 --> 00:29:05.285
This one's cute.

640
00:29:07.079 --> 00:29:08.622
That was kinda rough.

641
00:29:08.705 --> 00:29:11.208
I mean. I just steered us
through a crisis.

642
00:29:11.291 --> 00:29:12.918
Is it so hard to acknowledge that?

643
00:29:13.001 --> 00:29:15.921
Oh. yes. The girl who likes validation.

644
00:29:16.004 --> 00:29:17.130
Did your parents hang

645
00:29:17.214 --> 00:29:19.091
all of your finger paintings
up on the fridge?

646
00:29:19.174 --> 00:29:20.342
No. It's not that.

647
00:29:20.425 --> 00:29:21.677
I don't need a pat on the head.

648
00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:24.221
I-I-I need to know what she wants.

649
00:29:24.304 --> 00:29:26.431
All right. I need constructive feedback.

650
00:29:26.515 --> 00:29:27.808
Oh. feedback.

651
00:29:27.891 --> 00:29:30.811
And a lollipop. or... just feedback?

652
00:29:32.229 --> 00:29:33.438
Did you need this job?

653
00:29:34.564 --> 00:29:36.775
Did you take the job?

654
00:29:36.858 --> 00:29:38.860
So figure out a way to do this job.

655
00:29:38.944 --> 00:29:40.404
-Bye.
-Bye.

656
00:29:40.487 --> 00:29:42.155
[Nigel] Bye.

657
00:30:13.103 --> 00:30:15.105
Okay. so I think that if
we're talking about...

658
00:30:15.188 --> 00:30:16.523
Kind of follow that sartorial thing

659
00:30:16.606 --> 00:30:17.816
and how it links up to...

660
00:30:31.705 --> 00:30:32.789
[groans]

661
00:30:43.133 --> 00:30:44.926
What is this?

662
00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:46.386
Oh. that's far too pink. Ken.

663
00:30:46.470 --> 00:30:47.512
We are subtle.

664
00:30:47.596 --> 00:30:49.056
We're not Valentino.
do you know what I mean?

665
00:30:49.139 --> 00:30:51.433
Is this the sample I sent you?
"Tulip whisper."

666
00:30:51.516 --> 00:30:52.517
That's the one.

667
00:30:53.602 --> 00:30:55.812
Now I don't know. I don't know about that.

668
00:30:55.896 --> 00:30:57.147
Wow.

669
00:30:57.230 --> 00:30:58.774
Oh. there she is.

670
00:30:59.524 --> 00:31:00.525
Is this all you?

671
00:31:00.609 --> 00:31:02.736
-It is.
-It's impressive.

672
00:31:02.819 --> 00:31:05.155
You are very busy.
Thank you for your time.

673
00:31:05.238 --> 00:31:08.575
The thinking is that we will
cover the new flagship.

674
00:31:08.658 --> 00:31:10.285
and your role in putting it together.

675
00:31:10.369 --> 00:31:11.495
Is that Miranda's idea?

676
00:31:11.578 --> 00:31:13.580
Um. it was mine.

677
00:31:13.663 --> 00:31:15.624
Fine. If that's what you think
is best. then that...

678
00:31:15.707 --> 00:31:16.833
That's fine.

679
00:31:16.917 --> 00:31:18.335
Okay. great. Why don't you.
uh. give me a tour.

680
00:31:18.418 --> 00:31:19.461
Okay. Will do.

681
00:31:19.544 --> 00:31:22.130
Ken. it is not a whisper.
It's a cry for help.

682
00:31:22.214 --> 00:31:24.800
This. of course. is a tribute
to the grand staircase

683
00:31:24.883 --> 00:31:28.595
at the original Dior Atelier
on Avenue Montaigne.

684
00:31:28.678 --> 00:31:30.639
-[Andy] This is incredible.
-I know.

685
00:31:30.722 --> 00:31:32.224
And the cost?

686
00:31:32.307 --> 00:31:34.059
I'm not at liberty to discuss that. Andy.

687
00:31:34.142 --> 00:31:36.186
-but it is whopping.
-[snorts]

688
00:31:36.269 --> 00:31:38.021
What? What is that sickening look?

689
00:31:38.105 --> 00:31:41.566
It's just... It's nice
to see you all grown up.

690
00:31:41.650 --> 00:31:43.568
Ugh. So... So gooey.

691
00:31:43.652 --> 00:31:46.029
Always when you used to make that face...

692
00:31:46.113 --> 00:31:47.572
Because you were always so irritating.

693
00:31:47.656 --> 00:31:49.616
Yeah. you were a barrel of fun.

694
00:31:49.699 --> 00:31:51.743
No. hey. hey. hey. Listen.

695
00:31:51.827 --> 00:31:53.286
I don't know. It's nice to see you again.

696
00:31:53.370 --> 00:31:54.996
-Catch me up. Who are you now?
-No.

697
00:31:55.080 --> 00:31:58.458
Like. not for this. just. human to human.

698
00:31:58.542 --> 00:32:00.544
Fine. I am divorced

699
00:32:00.627 --> 00:32:02.254
from a pathological narcissist. Thank God.

700
00:32:02.337 --> 00:32:03.422
But I've got two beautiful children.

701
00:32:03.505 --> 00:32:04.339
Bronwyn and Roark.

702
00:32:04.423 --> 00:32:06.091
One at Collegiate. one at Chapin.

703
00:32:06.174 --> 00:32:07.843
-[Andy] Good for you.
-I know. Gorgeous. right?

704
00:32:07.926 --> 00:32:09.219
-Yeah.
-You?

705
00:32:09.302 --> 00:32:12.472
I... left New York for 15 years.

706
00:32:12.556 --> 00:32:16.101
Just lived everywhere. chasing stories.

707
00:32:16.184 --> 00:32:19.604
And I'm not married.
Never found the right person.

708
00:32:19.688 --> 00:32:23.066
And my kids are
at a doctor's office on 85th.

709
00:32:23.150 --> 00:32:24.276
They're frozen eggs right now.

710
00:32:24.359 --> 00:32:25.902
but I like to think of them
as my little ones.

711
00:32:25.986 --> 00:32:27.195
Siobhan and Esther.

712
00:32:27.279 --> 00:32:28.405
[strained chuckle]

713
00:32:28.488 --> 00:32:29.656
Okay. why did you leave Runway?

714
00:32:29.739 --> 00:32:31.199
I'm sorry. is this an expose?

715
00:32:31.283 --> 00:32:33.118
-No. it's just a question.
-It's not really relevant.

716
00:32:33.201 --> 00:32:35.996
Well. I mean. you worshipped
that place. and Miranda.

717
00:32:36.079 --> 00:32:37.622
So. why'd you leave?

718
00:32:37.706 --> 00:32:39.291
Why are you even asking me why?

719
00:32:39.374 --> 00:32:41.126
I mean. have a perv around this place.

720
00:32:41.209 --> 00:32:44.337
Luxury retail is the only
sector of the fashion business

721
00:32:44.421 --> 00:32:46.506
that still makes money. That's it. Retail.

722
00:32:46.590 --> 00:32:47.591
Everything else. forget it.

723
00:32:47.674 --> 00:32:50.135
So. yes. Happy to be here. frankly.

724
00:32:50.218 --> 00:32:51.678
Because do you know that 20 years ago.

725
00:32:51.761 --> 00:32:54.306
a $100 handbag was considered a splurge?

726
00:32:54.389 --> 00:32:56.308
Brands like us. we changed all that.

727
00:32:56.391 --> 00:33:00.061
Used logos and branding
because everyone understands.

728
00:33:00.145 --> 00:33:01.396
everyone gets it.

729
00:33:01.480 --> 00:33:04.316
that your bag. your scarf.
your perfume. your umbrella...

730
00:33:04.399 --> 00:33:05.567
Write this down.

731
00:33:05.650 --> 00:33:08.570
It tells the world who you are.
what you care about.

732
00:33:08.653 --> 00:33:11.114
And now. there's housewives in Banff

733
00:33:11.198 --> 00:33:12.199
who wouldn't dream of going out

734
00:33:12.282 --> 00:33:13.950
without one of our $3.000 totes.

735
00:33:14.034 --> 00:33:14.993
And that's a good thing?

736
00:33:15.076 --> 00:33:16.077
I don't know. Is it a bad thing

737
00:33:16.161 --> 00:33:17.704
to bring beauty and design to everyone?

738
00:33:17.787 --> 00:33:20.081
Everyone who has $3.000.

739
00:33:20.165 --> 00:33:22.292
Have you heard of Christmas?

740
00:33:22.375 --> 00:33:24.920
Anyway. don't you worry
about me and my career.

741
00:33:25.003 --> 00:33:26.630
Worry about yourself.

742
00:33:26.713 --> 00:33:28.840
I haven't seen any of your stories
get any traction.

743
00:33:28.924 --> 00:33:31.426
Nothing moving the needle there.

744
00:33:31.510 --> 00:33:32.928
What does Miranda say about it?

745
00:33:33.929 --> 00:33:35.096
Is she being tough on you?

746
00:33:37.557 --> 00:33:39.559
It's normal Miranda stuff.

747
00:33:39.643 --> 00:33:45.106
Yes. Irv. Yes. I am looking at
the numbers as we speak.

748
00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:46.191
[Irv] They're unacceptable.

749
00:33:46.274 --> 00:33:47.776
-Take care of it!
-Okay. I--

750
00:33:47.859 --> 00:33:48.860
[beeping]

751
00:33:50.195 --> 00:33:51.738
[sighs]

752
00:33:51.821 --> 00:33:53.532
Well. that sounded like a warm bath.

753
00:33:53.615 --> 00:33:57.285
[sighs] It's as if he forgets
he hired her.

754
00:33:58.036 --> 00:34:00.914
His hire. my problem.

755
00:34:00.997 --> 00:34:04.751
Did he say anything at all
about the global editorial job?

756
00:34:04.834 --> 00:34:07.254
No. he's gone mum about all that.

757
00:34:07.337 --> 00:34:09.965
I've spent my career trying to figure out
what people need to know.

758
00:34:10.048 --> 00:34:11.800
now I need to figure out
what people wanna click on.

759
00:34:11.883 --> 00:34:12.884
It's just...

760
00:34:12.968 --> 00:34:14.135
Maybe you just have to figure out

761
00:34:14.219 --> 00:34:15.804
how to do those things at the same time.

762
00:34:15.887 --> 00:34:18.682
You know. like. the smart stuff
and the fun stuff.

763
00:34:19.224 --> 00:34:21.017
Oh. my God.

764
00:34:21.101 --> 00:34:24.271
Hi. there. Hi.
Uh. would you like. uh. uh...

765
00:34:24.354 --> 00:34:27.649
No. Not going to sit.

766
00:34:27.732 --> 00:34:30.443
Um. Irv called frustrated.

767
00:34:30.527 --> 00:34:33.905
Your pieces are not breaking through.

768
00:34:33.989 --> 00:34:35.865
Yeah. no. I... But I do have to say that

769
00:34:35.949 --> 00:34:37.534
I think that some of
these pieces are worthwhile.

770
00:34:37.617 --> 00:34:39.578
Worth whose while?

771
00:34:39.661 --> 00:34:43.456
I mean. that you want to
turn new readers on to Runway.

772
00:34:43.540 --> 00:34:44.833
that's great.

773
00:34:44.916 --> 00:34:47.419
But maybe you don't wanna turn
off the ones we already have.

774
00:34:47.502 --> 00:34:49.879
Yeah. um. I-I didn't wanna...

775
00:34:49.963 --> 00:34:52.757
But we do have some
important work coming up.

776
00:34:52.841 --> 00:34:55.093
-Hmm. Oh. good.
-Uh... Um. I...

777
00:34:56.928 --> 00:34:58.430
I'm doing a Sasha Barnes profile.

778
00:35:02.517 --> 00:35:06.688
You really have an interview set?

779
00:35:07.606 --> 00:35:09.232
Uh. close.

780
00:35:09.316 --> 00:35:11.651
I'm just locking in the details.
but I-I-I have an in.

781
00:35:11.735 --> 00:35:12.944
Hmm.

782
00:35:13.028 --> 00:35:14.654
Sasha Barnes hasn't done press

783
00:35:14.738 --> 00:35:15.989
-in three years.
-Three years.

784
00:35:16.072 --> 00:35:18.158
Bit of a holy grail interview-wise.

785
00:35:18.241 --> 00:35:20.076
It's what makes it so exciting.

786
00:35:20.160 --> 00:35:21.202
So what's your in?

787
00:35:22.120 --> 00:35:23.496
Oh ...

788
00:35:23.580 --> 00:35:24.998
I don't have one.

789
00:35:26.207 --> 00:35:27.751
-Here we go.
-No. it's...

790
00:35:27.834 --> 00:35:29.252
God. this house is just-- Wow.

791
00:35:29.336 --> 00:35:32.631
Even back then. they were
just so insanely rich.

792
00:35:32.714 --> 00:35:34.049
And just so I'm clear.

793
00:35:34.132 --> 00:35:35.759
you have no way to get to Sasha.

794
00:35:35.842 --> 00:35:37.302
but you told Miranda that you do?

795
00:35:37.385 --> 00:35:38.678
Correct.

796
00:35:38.762 --> 00:35:41.306
That's just standing a little
close to the fire. no?

797
00:35:41.973 --> 00:35:43.183
I am the fire.

798
00:35:43.975 --> 00:35:45.268
Don't say that to people.

799
00:35:49.105 --> 00:35:50.690
Oh. yeah. yeah. I know this piece.

800
00:35:50.774 --> 00:35:52.192
This is a Cecily Brown.

801
00:35:52.275 --> 00:35:55.487
It sold for over 600K back in 2009.

802
00:35:55.570 --> 00:35:58.031
Okay. Uh. do you know who sold it to them?

803
00:35:58.114 --> 00:35:59.324
Yeah. probably my friend Paula.

804
00:35:59.407 --> 00:36:00.742
She's Cecily's biggest dealer.

805
00:36:00.825 --> 00:36:01.910
Can she get me Sasha's number?

806
00:36:01.993 --> 00:36:04.120
Maybe. Let me call Paula.

807
00:36:05.288 --> 00:36:07.082
-Can you go faster?
-You gotta chill.

808
00:36:07.165 --> 00:36:08.249
-I know.
-Okay.

809
00:36:08.333 --> 00:36:09.959
Paula said you might be able
to get me a number

810
00:36:10.043 --> 00:36:11.086
for Sasha Barnes.

811
00:36:11.711 --> 00:36:13.922
Uh-huh. You can't.

812
00:36:14.839 --> 00:36:17.008
Dog trainer? I'll take it.

813
00:36:17.092 --> 00:36:19.135
I just wanna ask her some questions.

814
00:36:20.261 --> 00:36:22.097
Yes. no. I promise
it won't get back to you.

815
00:36:23.056 --> 00:36:24.849
[gasps] Thank you!

816
00:36:25.975 --> 00:36:28.687
Sasha? Hi. Andy Sachs from Runway again.

817
00:36:28.770 --> 00:36:30.689
Uh. would love to talk
when you have a sec.

818
00:36:30.772 --> 00:36:33.983
So call me anytime. Anytime.

819
00:36:34.067 --> 00:36:37.612
I'm always clutching my phone. Thank you.

820
00:36:38.822 --> 00:36:41.449
Sorry. I am so focused on
getting this story.

821
00:36:41.533 --> 00:36:43.284
-No worries.
-I've left Sasha 18 messages.

822
00:36:43.368 --> 00:36:45.203
and I've reached out to everyone she knows

823
00:36:45.286 --> 00:36:46.204
and has ever met.

824
00:36:46.287 --> 00:36:47.497
Love that for you.

825
00:36:47.580 --> 00:36:48.998
Sorry. where is this lunch place?

826
00:36:49.082 --> 00:36:50.333
Right here.

827
00:36:51.501 --> 00:36:53.712
Lily. What...

828
00:36:53.795 --> 00:36:56.256
Saw her on Zillow. Isn't she gorg?

829
00:36:56.339 --> 00:36:58.550
Anyway. even if I loved it.
I can't afford that.

830
00:36:58.633 --> 00:37:00.677
Sure you can. You're making
double your old salary.

831
00:37:00.760 --> 00:37:02.387
-For how long?
-Ah. come on.

832
00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:03.596
Let's just go take a sneak peek

833
00:37:03.680 --> 00:37:04.681
at a model unit. okay?

834
00:37:04.764 --> 00:37:05.890
[Andy sighs]

835
00:37:05.974 --> 00:37:07.892
I just want you to have
the apartment you deserve.

836
00:37:11.896 --> 00:37:15.316
[chuckles] I mean. you gotta admit...

837
00:37:16.735 --> 00:37:18.820
-Yeah. it's not bad.
-Uh-huh.

838
00:37:18.903 --> 00:37:20.488
I bet you wouldn't have to
bang on the faucet

839
00:37:20.572 --> 00:37:22.323
-to get running water.
-[chuckles]

840
00:37:22.407 --> 00:37:24.826
-The bar is so low.
-[both chuckling]

841
00:37:24.909 --> 00:37:26.161
I'm gonna go check out the bedroom.

842
00:37:33.877 --> 00:37:34.878
Oof.

843
00:37:36.212 --> 00:37:37.547
-Hi.
-Hey.

844
00:37:39.382 --> 00:37:40.842
[Andy] This apartment...

845
00:37:41.885 --> 00:37:42.969
It's pretty nice. huh?

846
00:37:43.887 --> 00:37:46.264
Uh. if you like this kinda thing.

847
00:37:46.347 --> 00:37:47.515
Renovated properties?

848
00:37:47.599 --> 00:37:49.225
Everything that's wrong with our society.

849
00:37:49.309 --> 00:37:51.144
Right. right. [chuckling] Totally. yeah.

850
00:37:51.227 --> 00:37:52.395
No. no. no. Don't get me wrong.

851
00:37:52.479 --> 00:37:55.857
I mean. I-I-I don't...
I do love new places.

852
00:37:55.940 --> 00:37:57.442
It just kinda breaks my heart

853
00:37:57.525 --> 00:37:59.235
when you find a historic building

854
00:37:59.319 --> 00:38:00.945
-and then they do... You know?
-This.

855
00:38:01.029 --> 00:38:02.739
Well. it's heartbreaking
and the other thing is.

856
00:38:02.822 --> 00:38:05.241
you know... Um. I did this.

857
00:38:05.325 --> 00:38:07.243
Yeah. this was... This was me.
This is my building.

858
00:38:07.327 --> 00:38:08.453
-[laughs]
-It's fine. It's fine.

859
00:38:08.536 --> 00:38:11.748
-No. Okay. Hi. I'm a jerk.
-Hello.

860
00:38:11.831 --> 00:38:13.917
-[chuckles] I'm Peter.
-Andy.

861
00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:16.628
Nice to meet you. I don't want you to
think that I'm. like. a greedy developer.

862
00:38:16.711 --> 00:38:17.796
'cause I'm not.

863
00:38:17.879 --> 00:38:20.799
I'm a contractor.
So. I just found the building

864
00:38:20.882 --> 00:38:22.300
and I cobbled together
a bunch of rich old people

865
00:38:22.383 --> 00:38:23.343
who are. you know.

866
00:38:23.426 --> 00:38:24.803
awful. disgusting. heartbreaking people.

867
00:38:24.886 --> 00:38:26.179
and they bought it.

868
00:38:26.262 --> 00:38:27.472
And I got to work on it and.

869
00:38:27.555 --> 00:38:28.807
you know. they give me
a little slice of that.

870
00:38:28.890 --> 00:38:30.225
Also. the city was gonna knock it down.

871
00:38:30.308 --> 00:38:31.643
-like. it was in bad shape.
-No.

872
00:38:31.726 --> 00:38:33.728
-No. it was in bad shape.
-No. Oh. no.

873
00:38:33.812 --> 00:38:35.146
-They had rats here.
-You saved the building.

874
00:38:35.230 --> 00:38:36.147
Well. hero is your word.

875
00:38:36.231 --> 00:38:37.816
I don't wanna play into that narrative.

876
00:38:37.899 --> 00:38:39.734
But there were rats here
that were basically men.

877
00:38:39.818 --> 00:38:41.486
-Excuse me. [giggles]
-Yeah.

878
00:38:41.569 --> 00:38:42.570
Um...

879
00:38:43.571 --> 00:38:45.073
-Are you okay?
-Yeah. I'm fine.

880
00:38:45.156 --> 00:38:46.241
-Okay.
-I'm just... Sorry.

881
00:38:46.324 --> 00:38:49.285
-Oh. hi. Uh. Lily. this is...
-Hello.

882
00:38:49.369 --> 00:38:51.412
-Peter Warburton. Yeah.
-Peter. Peter.

883
00:38:51.496 --> 00:38:52.997
-And I gotta...
-Yeah.

884
00:38:53.081 --> 00:38:54.582
-I gotta go to this thing.
-Mm-hmm.

885
00:38:54.666 --> 00:38:56.334
I'm so sorry I insulted
your building. Good luck.

886
00:38:56.417 --> 00:38:57.502
It's fine.

887
00:38:57.585 --> 00:38:58.962
Call you. Lily!

888
00:38:59.921 --> 00:39:01.756
-Huh.
-Huh.

889
00:39:01.840 --> 00:39:02.924
You two were having a moment.

890
00:39:03.007 --> 00:39:04.133
Think so?

891
00:39:04.217 --> 00:39:06.177
-What's your story?
-My story?

892
00:39:06.261 --> 00:39:07.679
-Single?
-Yeah.

893
00:39:07.762 --> 00:39:09.806
-Why?
-Uh. well...

894
00:39:09.889 --> 00:39:11.099
-Divorced?
-Yes.

895
00:39:11.182 --> 00:39:12.475
-Kids?
-No.

896
00:39:12.559 --> 00:39:15.770
-Do you have a card?
-Yeah. Yeah. There you go.

897
00:39:15.854 --> 00:39:17.063
I'll be checking your socials.

898
00:39:17.146 --> 00:39:18.356
Okay.

899
00:39:25.029 --> 00:39:25.864
[Charlie] Oh...

900
00:39:26.364 --> 00:39:27.365
[Andy] I...

901
00:39:28.324 --> 00:39:29.659
-Where is she?
-Why?

902
00:39:29.742 --> 00:39:30.994
Charlie the Chair. where is she?

903
00:39:31.077 --> 00:39:33.204
Okay. I'll look. Um...

904
00:39:39.419 --> 00:39:42.922
We were thinking about
Victorian and Edwardian dress.

905
00:39:43.006 --> 00:39:45.633
and the incredible undergarments

906
00:39:45.717 --> 00:39:49.721
and how they exaggerate
and enhance a woman's figure.

907
00:39:52.223 --> 00:39:53.224
You hate the bow.

908
00:39:53.308 --> 00:39:54.350
I don't like the bow.

909
00:39:54.434 --> 00:39:57.353
Michael. I told you she'd hate the bow!

910
00:39:57.437 --> 00:39:59.314
I got the holy grail.

911
00:39:59.397 --> 00:40:01.190
[♪ exciting music playing]

912
00:40:15.204 --> 00:40:18.374
[Miranda] Why grant an interview now?

913
00:40:18.458 --> 00:40:21.377
I didn't intend to live a public life.

914
00:40:21.461 --> 00:40:23.588
I got my PhD. taught anthropology

915
00:40:23.671 --> 00:40:25.757
while Benji was tinkering with code.

916
00:40:26.716 --> 00:40:29.469
I had no idea that would. you know.

917
00:40:29.552 --> 00:40:32.805
put me on a rocket ship
into this hall of mirrors.

918
00:40:32.889 --> 00:40:34.807
-[Miranda chuckles]
-To mix a couple of metaphors.

919
00:40:34.891 --> 00:40:37.101
But here I am. this estate.

920
00:40:37.185 --> 00:40:39.437
this art collection. and my goal is...

921
00:40:39.520 --> 00:40:43.733
Well. actually my obligation

922
00:40:43.816 --> 00:40:46.194
is to eventually give
everything I have away.

923
00:40:46.277 --> 00:40:50.323
Well. we're very grateful
you chose to sit down with us.

924
00:40:50.406 --> 00:40:51.407
Lately. I've noticed

925
00:40:51.491 --> 00:40:52.992
what you've been doing with your articles.

926
00:40:53.076 --> 00:40:55.578
There's more gravitas
and an actual point of view.

927
00:40:55.662 --> 00:40:57.080
[Miranda] Hmm.

928
00:40:57.163 --> 00:41:01.334
You know. you haven't asked me
a single thing about Benji.

929
00:41:01.417 --> 00:41:04.253
Either the original or the 2.0 version.

930
00:41:04.337 --> 00:41:06.130
[both chuckle]

931
00:41:06.214 --> 00:41:09.175
Why should you be defined
by that marriage?

932
00:41:09.258 --> 00:41:10.259
He isn't.

933
00:41:11.844 --> 00:41:13.638
You're right. I'm not.

934
00:41:15.390 --> 00:41:17.433
Nor will I be defined by my second.

935
00:41:18.476 --> 00:41:19.894
I'm engaged.

936
00:41:19.978 --> 00:41:21.896
I met someone who has his own life

937
00:41:21.980 --> 00:41:24.399
and doesn't give a shit about
all this fanfare around me.

938
00:41:24.482 --> 00:41:26.234
[chuckling] Engagement?

939
00:41:26.317 --> 00:41:27.360
[gasps]

940
00:41:27.443 --> 00:41:31.572
I'm... I did not have any idea.

941
00:41:31.656 --> 00:41:33.700
Well. no one does. You're the first.

942
00:41:33.783 --> 00:41:36.119
I mean. I'm not sure why people care.
but they seem to.

943
00:41:36.661 --> 00:41:38.079
Yeah.

944
00:41:38.162 --> 00:41:40.957
You know. go ahead and break the story.

945
00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:42.917
It's my little gift to you.

946
00:41:43.001 --> 00:41:44.002
[Nigel] This is huge.

947
00:41:44.085 --> 00:41:46.629
A fabulous bit of gossip

948
00:41:46.713 --> 00:41:48.089
that we are breaking

949
00:41:48.172 --> 00:41:51.592
about an iconic but elusive woman.

950
00:41:51.676 --> 00:41:52.802
Photos are gorgeous.

951
00:41:52.885 --> 00:41:54.971
Your article is great.

952
00:41:55.054 --> 00:41:57.181
My styling is genius.

953
00:41:57.265 --> 00:41:58.641
This is what Runway does best.

954
00:41:58.725 --> 00:41:59.934
What if Miranda doesn't like it?

955
00:42:00.018 --> 00:42:01.227
Don't be negative.

956
00:42:01.310 --> 00:42:02.687
Yeah. why would I be negative?

957
00:42:03.646 --> 00:42:04.647
Stop it.

958
00:42:08.443 --> 00:42:09.444
That.

959
00:42:10.486 --> 00:42:11.571
Brilliant.

960
00:42:11.654 --> 00:42:13.197
-Right?
-Yep.

961
00:42:13.281 --> 00:42:15.950
So. let's just...

962
00:42:17.326 --> 00:42:18.578
...run it as our main piece.

963
00:42:18.661 --> 00:42:20.163
Push it out to the subscribers.

964
00:42:20.246 --> 00:42:22.415
and. uh. pin it on top of
the socials as well.

965
00:42:22.498 --> 00:42:24.000
[Andy] That's great.

966
00:42:24.083 --> 00:42:25.293
[♪ playful music plays]

967
00:42:25.376 --> 00:42:27.378
[breathes deeply]

968
00:42:35.636 --> 00:42:38.056
Maybe his medication is
something he needs to look...

969
00:42:38.139 --> 00:42:39.891
I have no idea what's going on
with it myself.

970
00:42:43.895 --> 00:42:47.231
-Hi.
-Hi. You came back.

971
00:42:47.315 --> 00:42:48.566
I came back.

972
00:42:48.649 --> 00:42:50.151
[Peter] Good.

973
00:42:50.234 --> 00:42:51.527
Any apartments still available?

974
00:42:51.611 --> 00:42:52.987
I think there's. like. 15 or 20.

975
00:42:53.071 --> 00:42:54.906
-How many do you want?
-[chuckles]

976
00:42:55.823 --> 00:42:57.116
Let's start with one.

977
00:42:57.200 --> 00:42:59.202
[SZA "Saturn" playing]

978
00:43:00.703 --> 00:43:01.662
[Andy] I think it's good.

979
00:43:01.746 --> 00:43:03.456
but I also have the 11 other stories

980
00:43:03.539 --> 00:43:06.501
that I had to post today
about. uh. coconut water

981
00:43:06.584 --> 00:43:08.920
and private jokes
between you and your lover

982
00:43:09.003 --> 00:43:10.630
and enzyme peels.

983
00:43:10.713 --> 00:43:11.964
Enzyme peels?

984
00:43:12.048 --> 00:43:13.132
That's the one.

985
00:43:13.216 --> 00:43:14.967
What is that? What does
an enzyme peel... peel?

986
00:43:15.051 --> 00:43:16.886
It peels the skin off your face.

987
00:43:16.969 --> 00:43:18.096
-Does it now?
-Right off.

988
00:43:18.179 --> 00:43:20.640
-Wow. People pay for that?
-[chuckles]

989
00:43:20.723 --> 00:43:21.933
Maybe I should.
I've still got all my old skin.

990
00:43:22.016 --> 00:43:23.726
Oh. no. that's completely out. No.

991
00:43:23.810 --> 00:43:24.936
I feel like I'm out.

992
00:43:25.019 --> 00:43:26.020
You're done. It's over.

993
00:43:26.104 --> 00:43:27.855
But you still get to do real journalism.

994
00:43:27.939 --> 00:43:29.065
Please. tell me that you do.

995
00:43:29.148 --> 00:43:31.776
You know what was amazing?

996
00:43:31.859 --> 00:43:34.070
The series you did on the Federal Reserve.

997
00:43:34.153 --> 00:43:35.822
um. when you were at the Vanguard.

998
00:43:35.905 --> 00:43:37.156
[chuckling] Oh. stop. stop.

999
00:43:37.240 --> 00:43:39.075
You read a four-piece series

1000
00:43:39.158 --> 00:43:40.827
about the inner workings
of the Federal Reserve

1001
00:43:40.910 --> 00:43:42.620
without knowing if you were
ever gonna see me again?

1002
00:43:42.703 --> 00:43:44.831
I did. And that's weird that I did that.

1003
00:43:44.914 --> 00:43:47.208
Now it's... Hearing you say it.
that is weird.

1004
00:43:47.291 --> 00:43:48.292
I shouldn't have told you that.

1005
00:43:48.376 --> 00:43:49.961
It's nice.

1006
00:43:50.044 --> 00:43:51.170
It's really nice.

1007
00:43:51.254 --> 00:43:53.464
I have been in long-term
relationships with guys

1008
00:43:53.548 --> 00:43:56.467
who have never read anything
of mine. so it's very nice.

1009
00:43:56.551 --> 00:43:57.677
That's appalling.

1010
00:43:57.760 --> 00:43:59.178
I know. It's crazy. But...

1011
00:44:01.055 --> 00:44:02.932
That's... [sighs] I can't help it.

1012
00:44:03.015 --> 00:44:06.185
Part of me does hope that
this is a stint at Runway.

1013
00:44:06.269 --> 00:44:08.271
and that it's a stepping-stone.

1014
00:44:08.354 --> 00:44:10.898
that I get to go back
and do real journalism

1015
00:44:10.982 --> 00:44:12.066
and. you know.

1016
00:44:12.150 --> 00:44:14.527
work for a paper
or a serious-minded magazine.

1017
00:44:14.610 --> 00:44:16.237
Or write a book.

1018
00:44:16.320 --> 00:44:18.072
A book? Think you got a book in you?

1019
00:44:18.156 --> 00:44:19.198
[chuckles]

1020
00:44:19.282 --> 00:44:20.700
I actually have an offer for a book.

1021
00:44:20.783 --> 00:44:21.868
That's awesome.

1022
00:44:21.951 --> 00:44:23.077
My friend wants me to write a book

1023
00:44:23.161 --> 00:44:25.288
about someone famous.

1024
00:44:25.371 --> 00:44:29.083
Okay? But she wants
the trashy tabloid version.

1025
